 $x\sqrt x=x· x^{\frac12}=x^{\frac32}.$Let's take a look at $3^\mathrm{rd}$root of $x^{\frac32}$ first
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt[3]{x^\frac32}&=\left(x^\frac32\right)^\frac13=x^\frac12\\
 \implies\quad{x\sqrt x\ ^{[third]}}&=(x^\frac32)^\frac13=x^{\frac12}\\ \implies\quad{x\sqrt x\ ^{[third]}}^\frac13&=\ Kate
\end{aligned}
\begin{aligned}\therefore\quad x\sqrt x\ &=(x\sqrt x\ ^{[third]})^3\ = (x^{\frac12})^3=x\end{aligned}
 <answer>Therefore the answer is $x$</answer>

Let's denote the equation of the tangent line to $g = y^2 = 4x$ as $f = y = mx + b$. It is tangent to this i.e. $g$ at some point $P$ with coordinates $(x_0, y_0)$. Since $(1,0)$ is the slope of  tangents to $g$ from $f$, the slope of line $f$ is also $4 \cdot 1 = 4$. Therefore, $m = 4$.
Since $P$ is on both $f$ and $g$, it follows that:
\begin{align*}
y_0 &= 4x_0 + b\\
y_0^2 &= 4x_0
\end{align*}
This implies that since $y_0^2 = (4x_0 + b)^2$:
\begin{align*}
16x_0 + 8b &= 0 \\
8b &= -16x_0
\end{align*}
Thus we denote the equation $b = -2x_0$.
Let's substitute the coordinates of $P$ into the equation $g(y)$. This implies that:
\begin{align*}
y_0 = (4x_0 + b) \quad * \quad y_0^2 = 4x_0
\end{align*}
Let's find the $x_0.$
\begin{align*}
(4x_0 + b)^2 &= 4x_0\\
(4x_0 - 2x_0)^2 &= 4x_0\\
9x_0^2 = 4x_0 \Longrightarrow 8x_0 + 81x_0 = 0 \Longrightarrow x_0 = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad x_0 = -81/8
\end{align*}
Now we have the $x_0,$ thus we have $m$ and $b$.
\begin{align*}
m = 4\\
b = -2 \cdot 0 = 0\\
b = -2 \cdot (-81 / 8) = 162 / 8
\end{align*}
<answer>
\begin{align*}
b = 20.25
\end{align*}


Thus, we have: $$y = 4x + 20.45$$
